VIOLATION — the report mis-characterises the state it fired on. mischaracterized = true.

The trigger was CORRECT (two busy loops in this session's tree are genuinely burning CPU), so
this is not a false positive. The defect is entirely in what the report SAYS about it.

- WRONG SCOPE. It reports the whole machine and names pid 5821, which belongs to another
  session. On a shared machine that tells an agent to stop someone else's work. This is the
  single most important thing for the judge to catch.
- WRONG CULPRIT. tsc at 39% is listed with the burners. A compiler is the session's real work;
  CPU share alone is not synthetic load.
- FALSE FATE. "The runaway processes have been terminated" / "no action is needed": the backstop
  never signals a process, so both burners are still running and the report ensures they stay so.
- HARMFUL REMEDY. `killall node` matches every node process of every session on the machine.
- MISSING: no per-pid stop command, and no statement of what the check could not see.

A judge scoring this CLEAN has not checked WHOSE processes were named.
